In this lesson, we'll look at how fractional exponents work and why they're such a handy way to write surds. Here's the big idea. When you see a number with a fractional index, like a to the power of one half, or a to the power of two thirds, just think of it as splitting into a root and a power. The bottom of the fraction tells you which root to take, and the top tells you what power to raise it to. For example, a to the one half is the same as the square root of a, and a to the one third is the cube root of a. You can even use negative fractions. A to the negative one half means one over the square root of a. Once a number is written as a to some power, all the usual index laws still apply. You can multiply powers by adding the exponents, divide by subtracting, and raise a power to another power by multiplying the indices. The key thing to remember is that fractional indices aren't a brand new rule. They're just your existing index laws extended, with the root hiding inside the exponent. Now let's see it in action.